{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"sed -n '405,437p' paper.tex; sed -n '1,155p' source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py\"","aggregated_output":"\\subsection{Public snapshots, normalization, and reproduction map}\\label{sec:repro}\nThe Open LLM contents revision is\n\\begin{center}\\small\\texttt{9c09a7cae43334062a82cb164f2ef255013dafa2}.\\end{center}\nThe HELM source is the public bucket's MMLU release v1.13.0. The accompanying frozen resources retain upstream URLs, upstream byte checksums, original source-row indices, and known aliases. The acquisition code canonicalizes Unicode/whitespace and raw score content before split assignment. Raw content and known alias clusters are disjoint across the recorded split; unknown lineage is not covered by that check. HELM is already on a unit scale; Open LLM uses the published normalized columns divided by 100, without treating raw columns as additional tasks. This choice makes the response cap relative to the published task scale. A different nonlinear score calibration would define a different intervention problem.\n% @evidence[[data/openllm.json#/rows/0/provenance/0]]\n% @evidence[[data/helm.json#/rows/0/provenance/0]]\n% @evidence[[data/protocol.json#/normalization]]\n\n\\paragraph{Reproduction entry points.}\n\\path{scripts/build_results.py} reads the immutable evidence registry and frozen resource dimensions. It generates Table~\\ref{tab:budgets}, both quantitative figures, the complete supplementary tables, \\path{derived/allocations.csv}, and \\path{derived/summary.json}. It does not rerun the evaluator. \\path{scripts/reproduce_allocations.py} independently checks recorded selected subsets against the score tables without changing any measured result. \\path{scripts/acquire_scores.py} preserves the supplied upstream acquisition procedure for reacquiring score snapshots into a separate destination. The complete commands, dependencies, checksums, and generation map are in \\path{REPRODUCIBILITY.md}. The conceptual overview is a separate deterministic rendering step owned by the document pipeline, not a measured plot.\n\n\\paragraph{Readable implementation evidence.}\nThe supplied selected entry point is specified in\n\\path{source_evidence/downstream/program/program_spec.toml}.\nIn \\path{source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py}, the rank totals begin at line 50, mean allocation at line 73, odd median at line 82, even median at line 93, scalar validation at line 129, Borda search at line 160, and rival-aware Borda allocation at line 193. The response cap is formed at line 260; request validation and deadline allocation begin at line 268. The original comparator entry point and source are retained in the adjacent baseline directory; its allocation routine begins at line 59. Source hashes identify these files but are not used as substitutes for inspecting the implementation.\nThe retained entry-point file is byte-identical to the downstream selected source. The final manifest records a separate tree digest; different digest scopes are not equated to a file-identity proof. Detailed settings above describe the inspectable selected source, while numerical conclusions rest on the independent recorded allocations and bounds. The retained evaluator source is byte-identical to the evaluator digest in the final manifest.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/baseline/src/program.py]]\n\n\\paragraph{Provenance and licenses.}\nLocal resource hashes, upstream source-file hashes, and transferred-array hashes identify different objects and are not interchangeable. The checksum manifest records each scope explicitly. Public access is not an explicit score-data redistribution license; neither the evaluation code license nor a model license automatically covers these tables. Reacquisition with attribution is documented, and public redistribution should be reviewed separately. Literature consultation used exact task-allowed identifiers and exact foundational identifiers, without following related-paper recommendations or citation graphs. Only consulted content supports citations; off-topic initial metadata matches were discarded. No prohibited work was intentionally retrieved or identified, and no claim depends on inferring its identity.\n\n\\subsection{Selection scope and independent final assessment}\nThe frozen configuration had no positive difference from the original baseline on the aggregate development selection score. The selection record contains three scored configurations and no unscored configuration; it does not establish a qualifying development improvement. The separate frozen final evaluation is completed and qualified, with independently checked original decisions and controls as described above. These statements use the selection record for development facts and the independent final assessment for qualification facts. There is no matched final original-baseline delta, and the weighted final evaluation score is not subtracted from the development score. Neither assessment status nor publication suitability changes the scope of the measured score-space conclusions.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/downstream_study/attempt_counts]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/downstream_study/search_score_delta]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/status]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/outcome]]\n\n\\subsection{LLM Usage}\nLLM assistance was used for research framing, solver-code development, reference-query planning, manuscript strategy, source inspection, extraction and plotting code, and drafting and revising this manuscript. The recorded evaluations supply the numerical evidence; this writing step does not replace those results with model-generated measurements. LLM-assisted scripts extract the existing records, generate the displayed tables and plots, and check evidence links and LaTeX consistency. No model training or new evaluation is reported as part of manuscript preparation. Claims about model capability, practical training cost, and unmeasured solver improvements are deliberately excluded. The retained logs document these roles; no unrecorded human verification or additional experiment is implied.\n\n\\end{document}\n\"\"\"Deterministic counterfactual allocation solver; no training or external files.\n\nProtocol: handle(dict) -> finite JSON dict. Extra request/response keys are forbidden.\nEvery episode starts with reset, clearing all arrays, transfer buffers and RNG.\nSeeds are arbitrary nonnegative integers and initialize RNG only, not workloads.\n\nreset: {op:'reset', seed:int} -> {ok:true}.\nupload_start: {op, encoding:'base64', dtype:'<f8', shape:[models,tasks],\n byte_length:int, chunk_count:int, sha256:64-char hex, task_ids:[str,...]}\n -> {ok:true}. Models 2..100000, tasks 2..100, bytes exactly models*tasks*8,\n logical limit 16 MiB; chunk_count=ceil(bytes/1048576), in 1..16.\nupload_chunk: {op, index:int, data:base64 str} -> {ok:true}; contiguous zero-based\n indices, each decoded block <=1048576 bytes, all except last full-sized.\nupload_commit: {op} -> {ok:true}; checks digest, finite [0,1] scores and shape.\n All buffering is candidate-owned memory; no host filenames or shared files.\nsolve: {op, targets:[distinct row int,...], rules:['mean','median','borda'],\n cap_fractions:[float,float], tolerance:1e-10, work_seconds:positive float}\n -> {solutions:[{status:str,tasks:[distinct column int,...]},...]}. At most\n eight targets; decisions are target-major, then rule-major, then cap-major.\n A selected coordinate is set to score + fraction*(1-score), fraction in [0,1].\n Both fractions can be zero for no-update experiments. Costs are one per\n selected coordinate, divided by task count for reported investment.\n Input scores/caps round to 10 decimal places before evaluation. Mean and\n median use float64; Borda is average pairwise win credit (ties half), updated\n competitors included. Co-first allows an aggregate deficit <= tolerance.\n status is 'feasible' (tasks witness), 'unattainable' (tasks=[]; full caps\n cannot win), or 'unresolved' (tasks=[]; honest bounded solver uncertainty).\n No optimality or internal-state self-report is accepted. Evaluator independently\n checks witnesses, ranks, unattainability and controls; no answers are returned.\n After one solve, release {op:'release'} -> {ok:true} clears episode buffers.\n No additional response fields, logs on stdout, file paths, polling, retries,\n state transfers, candidate-chosen objectives or hidden telemetry are supported.\n Maximum response: 48 decisions with at most 100 integer indices each, <64 KiB.\n reset -> upload_start -> upload_chunk(s) -> upload_commit -> solve -> release.\n A method may replace the solver below while preserving this exact interface.\n\"\"\"\n\nimport base64\nimport hashlib\nimport itertools\nimport math\nimport random\nimport time\n\nimport numpy as np\n\n_state = {}\n\n\ndef _rank_totals(matrix):\n    result = np.zeros(matrix.shape[0], dtype=np.int64)\n    for column in matrix.T:\n        _, inverse, counts = np.unique(column, return_inverse=True, return_counts=True)\n        below = np.cumsum(counts) - counts\n        result += 2 * below[inverse] + counts[inverse] - 1\n    return result\n\n\ndef _answer(indices):\n    return {'status': 'feasible', 'tasks': sorted(int(column) for column in indices)}\n\n\ndef _exhaustive(original, caps, wins):\n    available = np.flatnonzero(caps > original).tolist()\n    for size in range(len(available) + 1):\n        for subset in itertools.combinations(available, size):\n            indices = list(subset)\n            if wins(indices):\n                return indices\n    return None\n\n\ndef _mean_budget(original, caps, wins):\n    order = np.argsort(-(caps - original), kind='stable')\n    for size in range(len(order) + 1):\n        selected = order[:size].tolist()\n        if wins(selected):\n            return selected\n    return None\n\n\ndef _odd_median_budget(original, caps, competitor, tolerance):\n    already_above = np.count_nonzero(original - competitor >= -tolerance)\n    promotable = np.flatnonzero(\n        (original - competitor < -tolerance) & (caps - competitor >= -tolerance)\n    )\n    needed = max(0, len(original) // 2 + 1 - already_above)\n    if needed > len(promotable):\n        return None\n    return promotable[:needed].tolist()\n\n\ndef _even_median_budget(original, caps, competitor, tolerance, wins):\n    half = len(original) // 2\n    endpoints = np.unique(np.concatenate((original, caps)))\n    best = None\n    for lower in endpoints:\n        upper_candidates = endpoints[\n            (endpoints >= lower)\n            & ((endpoints + lower) / 2 - competitor >= -tolerance)\n        ]\n        if not len(upper_candidates):\n            continue\n        upper = upper_candidates[0]\n        low_needed = max(0, half + 1 - np.count_nonzero(original >= lower))\n        high_needed = max(0, half - np.count_nonzero(original >= upper))\n        shared = np.flatnonzero((original < lower) & (caps >= upper))\n        low_only = np.flatnonzero(\n            (original < lower) & (caps >= lower) & (caps < upper)\n        )\n        high_only = np.flatnonzero(\n            (original >= lower) & (original < upper) & (caps >= upper)\n        )\n        if low_needed > len(shared) + len(low_only):\n            continue\n        if high_needed > len(shared) + len(high_only):\n            continue\n        shared_count = min(len(shared), max(low_needed, high_needed))\n        selected = shared[:shared_count].tolist()\n        selected += low_only[:max(0, low_needed - shared_count)].tolist()\n        selected += high_only[:max(0, high_needed - shared_count)].tolist()\n        if best is not None and len(selected) >= len(best):\n            continue\n        if wins(selected):\n            best = selected\n    return best\n\n\ndef _scalar_solve(matrix, target, rule, caps, tolerance, context):\n    original = matrix[target]\n    aggregate = np.mean if rule == 'mean' else np.median\n    rival_scores = context[rule]\n    competitor = max(rival_scores[:target].max(initial=-np.inf),\n                     rival_scores[target + 1:].max(initial=-np.inf))\n\n    def wins(indices):\n        attacked = original.copy()\n        attacked[indices] = caps[indices]\n        return float(aggregate(attacked) - competitor) >= -tolerance\n\n    if wins([]):\n        return {'status': 'feasible', 'tasks': []}\n    if not wins(list(range(len(original)))):\n        return {'status': 'unattainable', 'tasks': []}\n    if rule == 'mean':\n        selected = _mean_budget(original, caps, wins)\n    elif len(original) % 2:\n        selected = _odd_median_budget(original, caps, competitor, tolerance)\n    else:\n        selected = _even_median_budget(original, caps, competitor, tolerance, wins)\n    if len(original) <= 6:\n        reference = _exhaustive(original, caps, wins)\n        if selected is None or reference is None or len(selected) != len(reference):\n            selected = reference\n    if selected is None or not wins(selected):\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
